From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 20:41:14 +0200
Subject: net: phy: intel-xway: use static features for the integrated xRX PHYs

The PHY11G and PHY22F cores integrated into the xRX SoCs run firmware that
the SoC loads into them once their reset is released. Their hardwired
identification registers answer as soon as the MDIO master is alive, so the
core gets matched and probed while the rest of its register file is still
coming up, and in that window MII_BMSR reads back 0x0000 as a perfectly
successful transaction.

Since commit dcdecdcfe1fc ("net: phy: switch drivers to use dynamic feature
detection") these entries carry no .features, so phy_probe() derives the
abilities from that BMSR read. A 0x0000 leaves phydev->supported with no
speed or duplex link mode at all, and nothing re-reads the abilities later,
so the empty set stands for the rest of the boot. A MAC driver that
validates the PHY's link modes then refuses to connect to a PHY that is
perfectly healthy a few milliseconds on.

Dynamic detection buys nothing here: an integrated core's capability set is
fixed by the firmware variant the SoC loads into it, gigabit for PHY11G and
10/100 for PHY22F. Declare it statically. The external PEF 707x/PEF 7061
entries keep detecting, as they have no such boot window.

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-fable-5
Signed-off-by: Grische <github@grische.xyz>

diff --git a/drivers/net/phy/intel-xway.c b/drivers/net/phy/intel-xway.c
index afbcec711744..041a1201cd84 100644
--- a/drivers/net/phy/intel-xway.c
+++ b/drivers/net/phy/intel-xway.c
@@ -698,7 +698,7 @@ static struct phy_driver xway_gphy[] = {
 		.phy_id		= PHY_ID_PHY11G_VR9_1_1,
 		.phy_id_mask	= 0xffffffff,
 		.name		= "Intel XWAY PHY11G (xRX v1.1 integrated)",
-		/* PHY_GBIT_FEATURES */
+		.features	= PHY_GBIT_FEATURES,
 		.config_init	= xway_gphy_config_init,
 		.probe		= xway_gphy_probe,
 		.handle_interrupt = xway_gphy_handle_interrupt,
@@ -716,7 +716,7 @@ static struct phy_driver xway_gphy[] = {
 		.phy_id		= PHY_ID_PHY22F_VR9_1_1,
 		.phy_id_mask	= 0xffffffff,
 		.name		= "Intel XWAY PHY22F (xRX v1.1 integrated)",
-		/* PHY_BASIC_FEATURES */
+		.features	= PHY_BASIC_FEATURES,
 		.config_init	= xway_gphy_config_init,
 		.probe		= xway_gphy_probe,
 		.handle_interrupt = xway_gphy_handle_interrupt,
@@ -734,7 +734,7 @@ static struct phy_driver xway_gphy[] = {
 		.phy_id		= PHY_ID_PHY11G_VR9_1_2,
 		.phy_id_mask	= 0xffffffff,
 		.name		= "Intel XWAY PHY11G (xRX v1.2 integrated)",
-		/* PHY_GBIT_FEATURES */
+		.features	= PHY_GBIT_FEATURES,
 		.config_init	= xway_gphy_config_init,
 		.probe		= xway_gphy_probe,
 		.handle_interrupt = xway_gphy_handle_interrupt,
@@ -752,7 +752,7 @@ static struct phy_driver xway_gphy[] = {
 		.phy_id		= PHY_ID_PHY22F_VR9_1_2,
 		.phy_id_mask	= 0xffffffff,
 		.name		= "Intel XWAY PHY22F (xRX v1.2 integrated)",
-		/* PHY_BASIC_FEATURES */
+		.features	= PHY_BASIC_FEATURES,
 		.config_init	= xway_gphy_config_init,
 		.probe		= xway_gphy_probe,
 		.handle_interrupt = xway_gphy_handle_interrupt,
